** The `CVE-2022-22965` vulnerability allows an attacker to inject arbitrary code through the `HttpHeaders` object in the Spring Framework. When an HTTP request is processed, the Spring Framework creates a new instance of the `HttpHeaders` class to store the request headers. An attacker can manipulate the `HttpHeaders` object to inject arbitrary code into the request processing pipeline. This can be achieved by using the `set` method to add a custom header with a malicious payload.

For instance, using the Spring Framework version 5.3.20 or earlier, an attacker can use the following code to inject arbitrary code:
```java
HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
headers.set("X-Forwarded-Host", "http://example.com");
headers.set("X-Forwarded-Proto", "https");
request.setRequestHeaders(headers);
```
This code can be used to inject arbitrary code into the request processing pipeline and execute malicious code.

**Mitigation Strategy:** Apply security updates and patches to the Spring Framework to fix the vulnerability. Use a Web Application Firewall (WAF) to detect and block suspicious requests.

